%I A213901 #49 Sep 12 2026 23:18:31
%S A213901 5,7,29,31,79,103,127,149,151,173,197,199,223,269,271,293,317,367,439,
%T A213901 463,487,557,631,701,727,751,773,797,821,823,941,967,991,1039,1061,
%U A213901 1063,1087,1181,1229,1231,1279,1301,1303,1327,1373,1399,1423,1447,1471,1543,1613
%N A213901 Fixed points of a sequence that gives the minimum length of a chain of 1,2,1,2,1,... so that the equation n*[n,1,2,1,...,n] = [x,...,y] between terminating continued fractions has a solution with x >= n^2 and y >= n^2.
%C A213901 Let [...,...,...] denote terminating continued fractions. For each n, build the value (quotient) of the continued fraction [n,1,2,1,2,...,n] by inserting the first m terms of the repeated sequence 1,2,1,2,... Note that m may be odd, so the fraction may end with [...,1,n]. Multiply this value by n and convert the product back to a continued fraction [x,...,y]. Define the sequence of minimum m(n) such that x and y in this representation are both at least n^2.
%C A213901 This length sequence m(n) starts 3, 2, 3, 5, 11, 7, 7, 8, 11, 4, 11, 11, 7, 5, 15, 8, 35, 9, 11, 23, 19, 21, 23, 29, 11, 26, 7, 29, 11, ... for n >= 2.
%C A213901 It refers to the equations
%C A213901    2*[2, 1, 2, 1, 2] = [5, 2, 5],
%C A213901    3*[3, 1, 2, 3] = [11, 10],
%C A213901    4*[4, 1, 2, 1, 4] = [18, 1, 18],
%C A213901    5*[5, 1, 2, 1, 2, 1, 5] = [28, 1, 1, 1, 28],
%C A213901    6*[6, 1, 2, 1, 2, 1, 2, 1, 2, 1, 2, 1, 6] = [40, 2, 1, 1, 4, 1, 1, 2, 40],
%C A213901    7*[7, 1, 2, 1, 2, 1, 2, 1, 7] = [54, 8, 54],
%C A213901    8*[8, 1, 2, 1, 2, 1, 2, 1, 8] = [69, 1, 5, 1, 69],
%C A213901    9*[9, 1, 2, 1, 2, 1, 2, 1, 2, 9] = [87, 1, 1, 2, 3, 84],
%C A213901   10*[10, 1, 2, 1, 2, 1, 2, 1, 2, 1, 2, 1, 10] = [107, 3, 8, 3, 107],
%C A213901   11*[11, 1, 2, 1, 2, 11] = [129, 125],
%C A213901   12*[12, 1, 2, 1, 2, 1, 2, 1, 2, 1, 2, 1, 12] = [152, 1, 3, 1, 1, 1, 3, 1, 152],
%C A213901   13*[13, 1, 2, 1, 2, 1, 2, 1, 2, 1, 2, 1, 13] = [178, 1, 1, 14, 1, 1, 178],
%C A213901   14*[14, 1, 2, 1, 2, 1, 2, 1, 14] = [206, 4, 206], ...
%C A213901 {a(n)} contains the fixed points of the length sequence m, i.e., those n where m(n)=n.
%C A213901 What is surprising is that all these fixed points appear to be prime numbers. (Such a sequence of fixed points may be defined for any other pair (p,q) which defines continued fractions [n,p,q,p,q,...,n]. Here we are looking at p=1, q=2. The sequence m(n) related to the pair p=2, q=1 is 1, 2, 3, 5, 5, 7, 3, 8, 5, 4, 11, 11, 7, 5, 7, 8, ... for n >= 2.)
%C A213901 If we have any sequence of positive integers, we can consider the sequence of primes which divide some term of the sequence. In some cases, this sequence of primes could be finite. For the Fibonacci sequences, the sequence of primes is all primes. If we speak of Fibonacci sequences, we are referring to any sequence which satisfies the recursion relation f(n) = f(n-1) + f(n-2) with arbitrary initial conditions f(1), f(2). Each of these sequences has a set of prime divisors. None of these has the sequence of all primes as its prime sequence, but the intersection of all these sequences of primes is nonempty, and coincides with A000057.
%C A213901 From that perspective, the current sequence seems to relate to the prime divisors common to some family of generalized Fibonacci sequences f(n) = e*f(n-1) + g*f(n-2) for some fixed coefficients e and g.
%C A213901 From _M. Jeremie Lafitte (Levitas)_, Sep 07 2026: (Start)
%C A213901 This makes precise the relation to generalized Fibonacci sequences anticipated above by Art DuPre. Define U(k)=A002530(k), k>=0, beginning 0,1,1,3,4,11,15,41,... Put z_U(n)=min{k>=1: n | U(k)}. DuPre's minimum-length function satisfies m(n)=z_U(n)-1, n>=2.
%C A213901 Modulo n, the successive projective states [U(k+1):U(k)] in P^1(Z/nZ) are obtained from [1:0] by alternately applying [u:v] -> [u+v:u] and [u:v] -> [2u+v:u]. The first return time of these states to infinity [w:0], with gcd(w,n)=1, is z_U(n). Consequently, the terms of this sequence are precisely the n for which this first return occurs at time n+1.
%C A213901 The sequence U is the Lehmer sequence associated with parameters (R,Q)=(2,-1), with discriminant R-4Q=6. Since U(k) is odd for odd k, and 3 | U(k) exactly when 3 | k, any n satisfying n | U(n+1) is coprime to 6. The Lehmer maximal-rank criterion proves that every term of this sequence is prime.
%C A213901 Put W(k)=2^floor(k/2)*U(k). Then W(0)=0, W(1)=1 and W(k+2)=2*W(k+1)+2*W(k). For odd n, z_W(n)=z_U(n). With A={{2,2},{1,0}}, A^k*[1:0]=[W(k+1):W(k)]. Thus, for every odd prime p, p belongs to this sequence if and only if the map [u:v] -> [2*u+2*v:u] is a single cycle on P^1(F_p), traversing all p+1 projective points.
%C A213901 Equivalently, an odd prime p belongs to this sequence if and only if p divides a term of every sequence f(n) = e*f(n-1) + g*f(n-2) with e = g = 2, and positive integer initial values f(1) and f(2).
%C A213901 The characteristic polynomial of A is t^2-2*t-2, with discriminant 12 and det(A)=-2. A full traversal therefore forces (3/p)=-1 and (-2/p)=-1, and hence every term satisfies p == 5 or 7 (mod 24).
%C A213901 If U' denotes the recurrence obtained from the reversed pattern 2,1,2,1,..., then U'(2j)=2*U(2j) and U'(2j+1)=U(2j+1), that is A048788. Thus z_U'(n)=z_U(n) for odd n. There are no even fixed points, since both U and U' are odd at odd indices. Hence, the patterns 1,2 and 2,1 give different length sequences but have exactly the same fixed points.
%C A213901 (End)
%H A213901 Bill McEachen, <a href="/A213901/b213901.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..360</a> (terms 1..157 from Art DuPre)
%F A213901 With z_U as defined above, a(n) is the increasing enumeration of the set {k >= 2: z_U(k) = k+1}. - _M. Jeremie Lafitte (Levitas)_, Sep 07 2026
%e A213901 From _M. Jeremie Lafitte (Levitas)_, Sep 07 2026: (Start)
%e A213901 For n=5, values U(1),...,U(5)=1,1,3,4,11, none are divisible by 5, whereas U(6)=15 is. Thus z_U(5)=6 and m(5)=5, explaining both the first term 5 and the equation 5*[5,1,2,1,2,1,5]=[28,1,1,1,28].
%e A213901 After the rescaling above, the projective orbit modulo 5 is [1:0] -> [2:1] -> [1:2]=[3:1] -> [1:1] -> [4:1] -> [0:4]=[0:1] -> [3:0]=[1:0], which is a single cycle on P^1(F_5).
%e A213901 Similarly, U(8)=56 is the first U(k) divisible by 7, so z_U(7)=8 and m(7)=7. The corresponding orbit is infinity -> 2 -> 3 -> 5 -> 1 -> 4 -> 6 -> 0 -> infinity, which traverses all 8 projective points.
%e A213901 The congruence conditions above are necessary, not sufficient. For instance, 53 == 5 (mod 24), but z_U(53) = 18 != 54.
%e A213901 (End)
%o A213901 (PARI)
%o A213901 {a(n,p,q) = local(t, m=1,s=[n]); if( n<2, 0, while( 1,
%o A213901    if(component(Mod(m,2),2)==1, s=concat(s,p),s=concat(s,q));
%o A213901 t=contfracpnqn(concat(s,n));
%o A213901    t = contfrac(n*t[1,1]/t[2,1]);
%o A213901    if(t[1]<n^2 || t[#t]<n^2, m++, break));
%o A213901 m)};
%o A213901 for(k=1,4000,if(k==a(k,1,2),print1(a(k,1,2)","," ")));
%o A213901 (PARI)
%o A213901 {isA213901(n)=my(u=1,v=0,t); if(n<3 || n%2==0, return(0)); for(k=1,n+1, t=u; u=2*(u+v)%n; v=t; if(v==0, return(k==n+1)));0};
%o A213901 for(n=3,4000, if(isA213901(n),print1(n,", ")));  \\ _M. Jeremie Lafitte (Levitas)_, Sep 07 2026
